        "translations": {
          "en": {
            "title": "Shared proof: uniqueness of coefficients reconstructing all masks",
            "assumptions": [
              {
                "id": "finite-coalitions",
                "text_md": "The coalition is finite. The games and coefficient functions are arbitrary real-valued functions on finite subsets; continuity, differentiability, independence and a zero output baseline are unnecessary."
              },
              {
                "id": "finite-domain",
                "text_md": "The ambient universe may be infinite, but every coalition in a sum is finite. Paper applications fix a finite universe and use its powerset."
              },
              {
                "id": "all-masks",
                "text_md": "The same coefficients must reconstruct every finite subset in the domain, including the empty set. Reconstruction at one specified coalition is insufficient."
              }
            ],
            "definitions": [
              {
                "id": "interaction",
                "text_md": "The interaction is a finite alternating sum. Since the summation subset is contained in the target, the cardinality difference in its exponent is a nonnegative integer."
              },
              {
                "id": "reconstruct",
                "text_md": "Reconstruction sums all coefficients activated by the retained-variable set."
              },
              {
                "id": "finite-difference",
                "text_md": "The insertion difference compares retaining the distinguished variable with masking it. The insertion lemma below is used only when that variable is absent from the target."
              }
            ],
            "overview": "Prove converse Möbius inversion for arbitrary coefficients, then substitute the reconstruction equality at every mask and conclude pointwise equality.",
            "completion_scope": "Coefficient uniqueness and the converse inversion actually used by the library; finite-universe paper quantifiers are recorded by separate adapters.",
            "example_md": "For two variables with mask outputs 2, 5, 7 and 13, the dividends are 2, 3, 5 and 3. Their full sum is 13, the empty-mask sum is 2, and singleton reconstruction gives 5 and 7. If only the full output 13 were required, coefficients 0, 0, 0 and 13 would also fit that one mask but fail all others. Hence the universal mask requirement matters.",
            "caveats": [
              "Uniqueness concerns coefficients for one fixed game and the same AND activation rule. It does not assert uniqueness of every explanation framework, causal model or AND–OR decomposition.",
              "The source inducts on cardinality; the library proves converse inversion. This text fully explains the route actually used by the library.",
              "Every subset, including the empty one, must be reconstructed exactly. Fitting only the full input, or fitting approximately, does not satisfy the theorem."
            ],
            "proof_steps": [
              {
                "id": "step-1",
                "title": "Prove converse inversion at the empty set",
                "body_md": "First prove $I_{R_d}(S)=d(S)$ for an arbitrary coefficient function $d$, without using the reconstruction hypothesis. Induct on finite $S$ and keep $d$ universally quantified. At the empty set, $R_d(\\varnothing)=d(\\varnothing)$, and the empty interaction of this game has that same value. Thus the empty coefficient is fixed rather than an additional free parameter.",
                "justification": "The definitions and strengthened finite-set induction."
              },
              {
                "id": "step-2",
                "title": "The difference of a reconstruction is another reconstruction",
                "body_md": "For $i\\notin S$, define $e(U)=d(U\\cup\\{i\\})$. Every $T\\subseteq S$ excludes $i$, so splitting the subsets of $T\\cup\\{i\\}$ gives $R_d(T\\cup\\{i\\})=R_d(T)+\\sum_{U\\subseteq T}d(U\\cup\\{i\\})$. Subtraction leaves $R_e(T)$. This identity is used only for $T\\subseteq S$: when $i\\in T$, the two-family argument would no longer be disjoint.",
                "justification": "The subset partition, cancellation of finite sums, and the definition of the new coefficient function."
              },
              {
                "id": "step-3",
                "title": "Use local equality to prove the insertion step",
                "body_md": "The insertion lemma gives $I_{R_d}(S\\cup\\{i\\})=I_{\\Delta_i R_d}(S)$. The interaction on $S$ reads only values at subsets of $S$, where the previous step proves that $\\Delta_i R_d$ equals $R_e$. Hence its interaction equals $I_{R_e}(S)$. Apply the induction hypothesis for the arbitrary function $e$ to obtain $e(S)=d(S\\cup\\{i\\})$. This proves converse inversion on the inserted set.",
                "justification": "Insertion differences, congruence on the subsets actually used, and strengthened induction."
              },
              {
                "id": "step-4",
                "title": "Substitute the reconstruction hypothesis for every mask",
                "body_md": "Restore the assumption that the same $d$ satisfies $R_d(T)=g(T)$ at every finite $T$. For arbitrary $S$, converse inversion gives $d(S)=I_{R_d}(S)$. In this finite sum every argument $U$ is a subset of $S$, so the assumption replaces $R_d(U)$ by $g(U)$ term by term. The resulting sum is $I_g(S)$. Coefficients are not chosen afresh for each mask.",
                "justification": "Converse inversion and the reconstruction equality at each subset."
              },
              {
                "id": "step-5",
                "title": "Conclude function equality and check the application domain",
                "body_md": "Since $S$ is arbitrary, equality at every finite input gives $d=I_g$. The public result quantifies over all finite subsets of a universe that may itself be infinite; a paper application restricts this universe to its finite $N$. In particular, the empty mask forces $d(\\varnothing)=g(\\varnothing)$, and a singleton forces $d(\\{i\\})=g(\\{i\\})-g(\\varnothing)$. Higher coefficients are then fixed by the already determined proper-subset coefficients.",
                "justification": "Function extensionality and the finite-universe paper specialization."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
