            "title": "AOG: equivalent regrouping of shared AND subpatterns",
            "overview": "A parent pattern is the union of its children’s variable sets. The product of their binary triggers equals the parent trigger, so sharing and regrouping preserve the weighted root output.",
            "assumptions": [
              "Each parent pattern is the union of finitely many child variable sets. Triggers are containment indicators, and the original coefficients and linear root are retained. Children may be shared and their variables may overlap."
            ],
            "definitions": [
              {
                "id": "cvpr-objects",
                "body_md": "Fix a finite universe $N$, input $x$ and input baseline vector $r$. The mask $x_S$ retains the original coordinates in $S$ and uses this same baseline elsewhere. The model is $v:X\\to\\mathbb R$, the game is $g(S)=v(x_S)$ and the output baseline is $b=g(\\varnothing)$. The paper uses raw dividends $w_A=I_g(A)=\\sum_{U\\subseteq A}(-1)^{|A|-|U|}g(U)$. Every subset sum includes the empty set, so $w_\\varnothing=b$. Centering $g_0=g-b$ changes only the empty dividend. A zero raw output baseline is not assumed."
              }
            ],
            "proof_scope": "entire original result; source proof and repaired proof separately preserved",
            "proof_steps": [
              {
                "id": "aog-union",
                "title": "Combine two child AND triggers",
                "body_md": "Containment of $A\\cup B$ in $T$ is equivalent to containment of both $A$ and $B$. The union trigger is therefore the product of the two binary triggers. Overlap between the child variable sets is allowed because trigger values are zero or one.",
                "justification": ""
              },
              {
                "id": "aog-tree",
                "title": "Repeated merging preserves each pattern state and the root output",
                "body_md": "For a finite child family with variable sets $V_c$, the parent set is their union. Induct over the child family. The empty family gives an empty union and empty product, both with trigger 1. Inserting a child uses the two-set union identity and the induction hypothesis, proving the product identity. Child variables may overlap and a child may be shared by several parents. Applying such a grouping to every original pattern preserves each trigger; retaining its coefficient preserves the weighted root output.",
                "justification": ""
              },
              {
                "id": "aog-empty",
                "title": "Check an empty pattern and a shared-node example",
                "body_md": "A shared node with variable set $\\{5,6\\}$ groups the pattern $\\{4,5,6\\}$ into children $\\{4\\}$ and that node. With mask $\\{4,5\\}$ neither representation fires; with the full pattern mask both fire. The empty pattern and empty child product both have trigger 1. This proves equivalent regrouping only; optimality of the MDL greedy algorithm is separate.",
                "justification": ""
              }
            ],
            "completion_scope": "entire original result; source proof and repaired proof separately preserved",
