        "translations": {
          "en": {
            "title": "Binary addition–multiplication models: dividends are activated-term coefficients",
            "overview": "Coordinate masking at a zero input baseline turns each monomial into a pure AND response. Linearity then combines its exact dividend; the coefficient includes the original sample product.",
            "assumptions": [
              "Binary coordinates and input baseline zero; merge polynomial terms with identical variable sets. A zero input baseline does not imply a zero output baseline for general models."
            ],
            "definitions": [
              {
                "id": "cvpr-objects",
                "body_md": "Fix a finite universe $N$, input $x$ and input baseline vector $r$. The mask $x_S$ retains the original coordinates in $S$ and uses this same baseline elsewhere. The model is $v:X\\to\\mathbb R$, the game is $g(S)=v(x_S)$ and the output baseline is $b=g(\\varnothing)$. The paper uses raw dividends $w_A=I_g(A)=\\sum_{U\\subseteq A}(-1)^{|A|-|U|}g(U)$. Every subset sum includes the empty set, so $w_\\varnothing=b$. Centering $g_0=g-b$ changes only the empty dividend. A zero raw output baseline is not assumed."
              }
            ],
            "proof_scope": "entire original result; source proof and repaired proof separately preserved",
            "proof_steps": [
              {
                "id": "addmul-binary",
                "title": "Evaluate each term at the fixed binary sample",
                "body_md": "For a monomial at input baseline zero, retaining its entire variable set keeps every factor; otherwise some factor is replaced by zero. Thus the masked monomial equals its coefficient times the original sample product times the containment indicator. An original zero factor makes that coefficient vanish; the empty monomial has product 1. Combine original terms with identical variable sets first.",
                "justification": ""
              },
              {
                "id": "addmul-transform",
                "title": "Transform each pure AND response and combine by linearity",
                "body_md": "The finite addition–multiplication polynomial, evaluated on actual coordinate masks, is a sum of pure AND games by the preceding identity. Each has a dividend only at its own variable set. Finite linearity therefore makes a fixed set receive precisely its original coefficient times its sample product, if present in the polynomial, and zero otherwise.",
                "justification": ""
              },
              {
                "id": "addmul-examples",
                "title": "Check both source samples and the boundary",
                "body_md": "The source model is $v(x)=3x_1-2x_2x_3-x_3x_4x_5+5x_4x_6$. At the all-ones sample its nonzero dividends are 3, −2, −1 and 5 at the four displayed monomial sets. When the third coordinate is zero, only dividends 3 and 5 remain. Full outputs are respectively 5 and 8. Both examples have zero empty-mask output and empty dividend; these are exact activated-term coefficients.",
                "justification": ""
              }
            ],
            "completion_scope": "entire original result; source proof and repaired proof separately preserved",
            "original_statement_note": "The formulas and quantifiers were checked against the formal PDF. The Chinese source text is a translation rather than a verbatim English quotation.",
            "original_proof_note": "The author’s mathematical calculations and necessary argument are preserved, with printed errors retained. The formal PDF and page evidence preserve the original layout. Unnumbered arguments are distinguished from proofs not supplied by the authors."
          }
        },
