            "text_md": "原文仅说适用于continuously differentiable functions，Lemma1本身未假设解析性或Taylor级数等于函数。n=1,b=0,x=1，取v(t)=exp(−1/t²)（t≠0），v(0)=0。此函数C∞，所有在0的导数为0，但I({1})=e^(−1)≠0，而Eq8右边为0。反例针对一般Lemma1，不满足全空间高阶导数为零，故不直接否定1β⇒1α。",
            "translations": {
              "en": {
                "title": "The general infinite Taylor identity lacks a validity premise",
                "analysis_md": "The source says continuously differentiable functions without assuming analyticity or equality to a Taylor series. Take $n=1,r=0,x=1$ and $v(t)=e^{-1/t^2}$ for $t\\ne0$, $v(0)=0$. This smooth function has all derivatives zero at the baseline, but $I(\\{1\\})=e^{-1}\\ne0$, while Eq.(8)’s right side is zero. The counterexample concerns general Lemma 1 and does not satisfy the global derivative cutoff, so it does not refute the cutoff implication.",
                "text_md": "The source says continuously differentiable functions without assuming analyticity or equality to a Taylor series. Take $n=1,r=0,x=1$ and $v(t)=e^{-1/t^2}$ for $t\\ne0$, $v(0)=0$. This smooth function has all derivatives zero at the baseline, but $I(\\{1\\})=e^{-1}\\ne0$, while Eq.(8)’s right side is zero. The counterexample concerns general Lemma 1 and does not satisfy the global derivative cutoff, so it does not refute the cutoff implication.",
                "impact_md": "General Lemma 1 and the infinite expansions in Eqs.(2),(11) cannot be repaired by silently adding analyticity."
              }
            },
