                "id": "iclr2024-sparse-lemma1-step-1",
                "title": "Definitions, original target, and premise adapter",
                "body_md": "For nonempty S the author claims\n$I(S)=\\sum_{\\kappa\\in Q_S}D^\\kappa v(r)\\prod_{i\\in S}(x_i-r_i)^{\\kappa_i}/\\kappa!$,\nwhere $Q_S$ consists of multiindices positive on S and zero elsewhere. Define this right-hand side as $\\widetilde I(S)$ and set $\\widetilde I(\\varnothing)=0$. Group every masked Taylor expansion by its nonzero-coordinate support to obtain $v(x_S)=b+\\sum_{T\\subseteq S}\\widetilde I(T)$, then invoke coefficient uniqueness for all masks, including the empty mask. A single full-input equality cannot establish uniqueness.\nA smooth function need not equal its Taylor series: $v(t)=e^{-1/t^2}$ for nonzero t and $v(0)=0$ has all derivatives zero at 0, but $I(\\{1\\})=e^{-1}$. This refutes the unrestricted smooth-function reading; adding analyticity would change the original proposition. If Eq. (2) is instead a given valid Taylor representation, that example does not satisfy it and does not refute the conditional grouping argument. It also does not satisfy the global high-order zero-derivative Assumption 1-β, so it does not refute the next finite-order implication. The original formula and proof are retained.",
                "formula_tex": "I(S)=\\sum_{\\kappa\\in Q_S}\\frac{D^\\kappa v(r)}{\\kappa!}\\prod_{i\\in S}(x_i-r_i)^{\\kappa_i},\\quad S\\ne\\varnothing.",
