            "title": "Theorem 2: the complete literal AND-OR matching statement",
            "overview": "Arbitrary mask-label components reconstruct in their conditional games. Adding them recovers the original output even when distinct mask labels yield the same input.",
            "completion_scope": "complete arbitrary mask-label component statement, including every gamma assignment; actual input-function variant retained as a special case",
            "assumptions": [
              "$g(U)=v(x_U)$; finite $N$ and $T\\subseteq N$. AND and OR components are arbitrary set functions, with $g(U)=g_{and}(U)+g_{or}(U)$ for every label $U$. No assumption $x_i\\ne r_i$ or extension to input functions is required."
            ],
            "definitions": [
              {
                "id": "f11-coordinate-and-label-objects",
                "body_md": "Fix $N=\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$, a model $v:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$, an input $x$ and one coordinate baseline $r$. Write $g(A)=v(x_A)$ and $b=g(\\varnothing)$. Repeated masking defines $g_T(A)=v((x_T)_A)=g(T\\cap A)$. Define $I_g(S)=\\sum_{L\\subseteq S}(-1)^{|S|-|L|}g(L)$ and $w_A=I_g(A)$. For nonempty $S$, define $O_g^N(S)=-I_{g^c}(S)$ with $g^c(L)=g(N\\setminus L)$; separately set $O_g^N(\\varnothing)=g(\\varnothing)$. All subset sums include the empty set, and raw outputs need not have zero baseline. For components, use $v_{and}$ and $v_{or}$ to form their respective games and conditional games. Pages 4 and 6 allow arbitrary mask-label parameters $\\gamma_A$. Thus the most general joint decomposition consists of $g_{and}(A)=\\tfrac12g(A)+\\gamma_A$ and $g_{or}(A)=\\tfrac12g(A)-\\gamma_A$, or any two set functions summing to $g(A)$. Define $g_{and,T}(L)=g_{and}(T\\cap L)$ and similarly for OR. Here $v_{and}(x_A),v_{or}(x_A)$ denote mask-label values; they are actual evaluations on input vectors only when the components come from input functions. The case $x_i=r_i$ is allowed. Distinct labels need not give distinct inputs, and arbitrary gamma values need not extend to a single-valued input function."
              }
            ],
