        "translations": {
          "en": {
            "title": "F.8: conditional Shapley for the selected variables",
            "overview": "The other coordinates keep their original values, defining a conditional game whose player universe is Q.",
            "assumptions": [
              "The selected universe is a subset of the original universe; outside coordinates retain the original input and selected coordinates use their source baseline when masked.",
              "The source readout and R1/R2 hold at every mask of the original finite universe."
            ],
            "definitions": [
              {
                "id": "hnet-fixed-game",
                "body_md": "Fix a finite universe $N$, model $v$, input $x$ and input baseline vector $r$. The mask $x_T$ retains $x$ on $T$ and uses $r$ elsewhere. Set $g(T)=v(x_T)$, output baseline $b=g(\\varnothing)$ and centered game $g_0(T)=g(T)-b$. The paper's $V(T)$ is $g_0(T)$ and its $I(T)$ is $I_{g_0}(T)$, with empty interaction zero. For unit $j=(l,u)$, set $u_j(T)=z_j(x_T)$; its $J_j(T)$ is the interaction of $u_j-u_j(\\varnothing)$. The source's input baseline $b_i$ corresponds to $r_i$, and differs from the canonical scalar output baseline $b$."
              }
            ],
            "proof_steps": [
              {
                "id": "hnet-f8-game",
                "title": "Define every conditional mask",
                "body_md": "Fix the selected subset and keep all outside coordinates at their original input. Its mask T is the original mask with all outside coordinates retained. The conditional empty output is therefore the original output at that outside set. Center this conditional game at its own empty output. The experiment selects twelve players, while the argument applies to any finite selected subset. It differs from a game that masks every outside coordinate to the original baseline.",
                "justification": "",
                "lean_refs": []
              },
              {
                "id": "hnet-f8-field",
                "title": "Prove the reduced-field equivalence",
                "body_md": "A field contained in the original universe is retained by T together with the fixed outside coordinates exactly when its intersection with the selected universe lies in T. Forward, a selected field coordinate cannot lie outside the selected universe. Reverse, each field coordinate is either retained by T or is among the fixed outside coordinates. Substitution into the unit mask law gives the unanimity game on the reduced field.",
                "justification": "",
                "lean_refs": [
                  {
                    "declaration": "Harsanyi.Network.conditional_receptive",
                    "source_path": "lean/HarsanyiLib/Harsanyi/Extensions/HarsanyiNetwork.lean",
                    "scope": "actual_definition_or_theorem",
                    "explanation_md": "This declaration verifies the stated mathematical scope; correspondence to the source prose is checked separately.",
                    "line": 181
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "hnet-f8-shapley",
                "title": "Apply the actual classical bridge and check boundaries",
                "body_md": "Apply the proved mask-law Shapley theorem on the selected universe. Only a term whose reduced field contains the attributed player evaluates a denominator, making it positive. A field with empty selected intersection gives a constant conditional unit game and zero centered effects. Page 19 explicitly uses “number of selected variables” for its cardinality; the canonical quantity is the size of the reduced field, not the original field. Efficiency gives the difference between the full output and the fixed-outside output.",
                "justification": "",
                "lean_refs": [
                  {
                    "declaration": "PaperHarsanyiNet.finite_conditional_attribution",
                    "source_path": "corpus/public/reader/icml2023-harsanyinet/lean/PaperHarsanyiNet.lean",
                    "line": 58,
                    "scope": "original_finite_mask_domain",
                    "explanation_md": "This declaration verifies the stated mathematical scope; correspondence to the source prose is checked separately."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "completion_scope": "Complete conditional-game attribution derivation and actual factorial-definition Lean bridge, distinguished from attribution with every original player.",
            "proof_scope": "Complete conditional-game attribution derivation and actual factorial-definition Lean bridge, distinguished from attribution with every original player.",
            "original_statement_md": "F.8, formal PDF page 19. Complete author mathematical construction and displayed chain, with the original symbols preserved.\n\nTo reduce computational cost, the authors randomly sampled $n=12$ foreground variables as input variables on image datasets. Ground-truth Shapley values were computed by masking the selected twelve variables and keeping all other variables at their original sample values. The selected set is denoted $\\widehat N$, with $|\\widehat N|=12$. Specifically, the baseline is set by\n\\[\n\\forall i\\notin\\widehat N,\\ b_i=x_i,\\qquad\\forall i\\in\\widehat N,\\ b_i=0.\n\\]\nBased on this baseline sample, the authors obtained $2^{|\\widehat N|}$ different masked samples. For HarsanyiNet they computed the Shapley values for the selected variables based on Theorem 1, visiting sets containing a selected variable, written $S\\ni i,\\ \\forall i\\in\\widehat N$. The source explicitly says that $|S|$ denotes the number of selected variables in $S$. Its displayed construction is\n\\[\n\\varphi(i)=\\sum_{S\\subseteq N:\\,S\\ni i,\\,i\\in\\widehat N}\\frac{1}{|S|}I(S),\n\\qquad\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\varphi(i)=v(x_N=x)-v(x_\\varnothing).\n\\]\nFor the ShapNets comparison, the authors instead set each unselected variable's baseline to zero, producing $x'_i=x_i$ for $i\\in\\widehat N$ and $x'_i=0$ otherwise, and computed the selected-variable attributions of ShapNet on this masked input sample. This different comparison construction is preserved rather than conflated with the preceding conditional game.\n",
            "notation_map": [
              {
                "original": "V(T)",
                "canonical": "g_0(T)",
                "note": "The source reward is explicitly centered; no zero raw baseline is assumed."
              },
              {
                "original": "I(S)",
                "canonical": "I_{g_0}(S)",
                "note": "The source empty dividend is zero, and subset sums include the empty set."
              },
              {
                "original": "b_i",
                "canonical": "r_i",
                "note": "The input baseline vector differs from the scalar output baseline."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
